            <fr:title text="The pencil criterion">The pencil criterion</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:pencil.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M \in  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  M \in  V(J) \iff  M + t F(M)
  \text { is nilpotent for every } t \in  K.
]]></fr:tex>
Equivalently <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  M \in  V(J) \iff 
  \operatorname {span}_K \lbrace  M, F(M) \rbrace  \subseteq  \mathcal {N} :
]]></fr:tex>
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex> is the locus of matrices <html:em>nilpotent to all orders
in the Frobenius direction</html:em>. In particular
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(V(J)) = V(J)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Immediate from <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">the evaluation identity</fr:link>: the
coefficients of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi _{M + tF(M)}]]></fr:tex> are the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})(M)]]></fr:tex>, so all of them vanish exactly when every
member of the pencil is nilpotent.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">The evaluation identity</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">The Steenrod ideal</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0002</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The q-Steenrod operations">The q-Steenrod operations</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">Maithani 2025; L. Smith, <html:em>Polynomial Invariants of Finite Groups</html:em>, Ch.~11.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations: the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-algebra homomorphism <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P} \colon  S \to  S[T], \qquad  x \mapsto  x + x^q T
]]></fr:tex>
on linear forms, with <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(f) = \sum _{i \ge  0} \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) T^i]]></fr:tex>.
Each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}]]></fr:tex> raises degree by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i(q-1)]]></fr:tex> and preserves the
invariants of any degree-preserving action. For homogeneous <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> of
degree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>-coefficients, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) = 0 \text { for } i > d,
  \qquad  \mathcal {P}^{d}(f) = f^q.
]]></fr:tex>
These two vanishing facts are what make <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>
finitely generated in the stated degrees.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/sec-foundations/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sec-foundations</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-foundations/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › Foundations"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › Foundations</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>From the conjugation action to the statement of the height
  conjecture.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">The setup</fr:link> — the ambient objects, the conjugation
    action, and why it is modular.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0054/" title="The characteristic polynomial coefficients" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/" display-uri="ffi-0054" type="local">The characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:link> — the
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> invariants the whole program takes as input.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">The entrywise Frobenius</fr:link> — the direction everything
    deforms in.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local">The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations</fr:link> — the operations, and
    what they do to degrees.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">The evaluation identity</fr:link> — the one fact the program
    runs on.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0004/" title="Polarizations and the trace-form generators" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0004/" display-uri="ffi-0004" type="local">The polarizations</fr:link> — the sparse trace-form
    generators.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">The Steenrod ideal</fr:link> — the ideal whose height is in
    question.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0006/" title="The pencil criterion" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0006/" display-uri="ffi-0006" type="local">The pencil criterion</fr:link> — nilpotency to all orders in
    the Frobenius direction.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0007/" title="Flag witnesses for the partner variety" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0007/" display-uri="ffi-0007" type="local">The flag witnesses</fr:link> — the subvarieties that attain
    the lower bound.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0008/" title="The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0008/" display-uri="ffi-0008" type="local">The Krull bound</fr:link> — why the whole question is an upper
    bound.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">The height conjecture</fr:link> — the conjecture itself.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> — what is actually left to prove.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Related">Related</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0004/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0004</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0004/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Polarizations and the trace-form generators">Polarizations and the trace-form generators</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">journal sec:polarization.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For commuting indeterminates <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[s, t]]></fr:tex> and two generic matrices
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X, Y]]></fr:tex>, the <html:em>polarizations</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{a,b}]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex> are
defined by <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  c_{j}(sX + tY) = \sum _{a + b = j} s^a t^b P_{a,b}(X, Y),
]]></fr:tex>
bihomogeneous of bidegree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(a, b)]]></fr:tex>, with
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{j,0} = c_{j}(X)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{0,j} = c_{j}(Y)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j \le  4]]></fr:tex> these are explicit combinations of twisted trace
forms <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {tr}(X^{a_1} F(X)^{b_1} \cdots  X^{a_r} F(X)^{b_r})
]]></fr:tex>
— the sparse trace-form generators. Setting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y = F(X)]]></fr:tex> recovers the
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod powers</fr:link>, by
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">the evaluation identity</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local">The q-Steenrod operations</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">The evaluation identity</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0054/" title="The characteristic polynomial coefficients" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/" display-uri="ffi-0054" type="local">The characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">The entrywise Frobenius</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0054</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0054/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The characteristic polynomial coefficients">The characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:setup. The caution was added 2026-08-23, correcting a claim that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex> is generated by the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex> — never in the journal, introduced when the node was first written.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The characteristic polynomial of the generic matrix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \chi _{X}(\lambda ) = \lambda ^n - c_{1} \lambda ^{n-1}
  + c_{2} \lambda ^{n-2} - \cdots  + (-1)^n c_{n},
  \qquad  c_{j} \in  S^G, \quad  \deg  c_{j} = j.
]]></fr:tex>
So <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{1} = \operatorname {tr} X]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{n} = \det  X]]></fr:tex>. The signs are
irrelevant to everything downstream.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> These <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> invariants are the entire input to the program:
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link> is generated by their <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod
powers, and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0004/" title="Polarizations and the trace-form generators" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0004/" display-uri="ffi-0004" type="local">the polarizations</fr:link> are their bihomogeneous
parts.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Warning.</html:strong> The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex> do <html:em>not</html:em> generate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex>. They generate
the invariants of the <html:em>algebraic</html:em> group <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {GL}_n]]></fr:tex> acting by
conjugation; the group here is the finite <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G = \mathrm {GL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is
integral over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex>, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  S^G = n^2]]></fr:tex> while
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k[c_{1}, \dots , c_{n}]]]></fr:tex> has dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>. Already at
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 2]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q = 2]]></fr:tex> the pair <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(c_{1}, c_{2})]]></fr:tex> fails to
separate conjugacy classes: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex> and
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\begin {pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end {pmatrix}]]></fr:tex> agree on both. Only
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j} \in  S^G]]></fr:tex> is used anywhere in the program.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0002</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The q-Steenrod operations">The q-Steenrod operations</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="provenance">Maithani 2025; L. Smith, <html:em>Polynomial Invariants of Finite Groups</html:em>, Ch.~11.</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations: the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-algebra homomorphism <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P} \colon  S \to  S[T], \qquad  x \mapsto  x + x^q T
]]></fr:tex>
on linear forms, with <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(f) = \sum _{i \ge  0} \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) T^i]]></fr:tex>.
Each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}]]></fr:tex> raises degree by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i(q-1)]]></fr:tex> and preserves the
invariants of any degree-preserving action. For homogeneous <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> of
degree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>-coefficients, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) = 0 \text { for } i > d,
  \qquad  \mathcal {P}^{d}(f) = f^q.
]]></fr:tex>
These two vanishing facts are what make <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>
finitely generated in the stated degrees.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
